From 33cf027e923a42333264203838457e43acee94a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(inventory): translate server-authoritative Bedrock
 requests
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Port inventory actions and crafting data onto the existing container tracker instead of the parallel PR 276 container hierarchy. Track predicted changes by request ID, apply server responses to the owning container, and restore snapshots after rejected requests. Include focused response and routing tests.

Decode crafting recipe ingredients with the protocol 2193 CraftingData layout: a presence variant, string descriptor subtype, and signed varint count. The auto-crafting request uses a different numeric descriptor layout. Read shaped and shapeless recipes plus their optional unlocking requirements without desynchronizing login packets.

Pin inventory action, text origin, container, and inventory source values to Mojang beta protocol 2193. The upstream generated snapshots contain later enum renumbering; using them would send incorrect action and slot IDs. Verify the emitted bytes in codec tests.

Open moving storage vehicles through their entity container IDs and display their vehicle titles. Close unsupported chemistry screens on Bedrock so they cannot lock later inventory actions. Support creative edits across player inventory slots and translate drag distribution and double-click collection.

Recover unanswered requests by reopening the player inventory for authoritative contents and cursor state. Disconnect with an explicit error if recovery also times out, since stale stack network IDs would make further clicks unsafe.

Accept hotbar response slots by their Bedrock backing index before mapping them to Java menu slots. The old ownership check rejected slots 0 through 8, losing server corrections and stack network IDs after inventory actions. Clear stale enchantment options and validate the input, lapis, and network IDs before requesting an enchantment.

Keep Cereal’s union discriminator before the embedded action type byte. For actions after Create, the embedded legacy ID is two greater because Cereal omits two obsolete container actions. Bedrock rejected crafting output when both IDs were written as 10.

Keep FullContainerName's presence byte and optional dynamic ID. Although the published protocol 2193 schema shows a fixed-width ID, the live 1.26.51.1 server sends the shorter encoding; using four bytes consumed the following storage item in InventoryContent.

Encode stack request slot IDs as little-endian 32-bit integers and keep optional container dynamic IDs. The versioned Cloudburst implementation for protocol 2193 agrees, while the public beta schema lists a different Cereal variant. The earlier wire format reached server-side slot validation; tagged variable integers caused packet parser rejection.

Queue a bounded number of clicks while a request is pending, then drain them against updated network IDs after each response. Discard queued screen actions when their screen closes. Preserve independent player-inventory writes across normal close acknowledgements; recovery discards all queued actions.

Include the enchanted result item in each enchantment request, matching the native action sequence. Preserve every server-provided enchantment on that result so Bedrock can validate options with multiple effects.

Encode enchantment craft results with the request-specific named-item descriptor, including user data. The ordinary inventory item type starts with a numeric item ID and made Bedrock reject the request as malformed.

Write both the Cereal union variant and its embedded item descriptor type. Omitting the second marker made Bedrock parse the item name length as an enum and disconnect.

Classify actor-backed container opens using the target entity ID. Bedrock uses generic CONTAINER for storage vehicles; checking its reported position as a chest block closed the Java screen on the next inventory tick.

Match the native enchant request with result item data, input and material consumption, and output placement. Use the effective Java game mode when deciding whether to consume lapis: Bedrock Default can inherit survival from the level, and omitting lapis then makes the server reject the request.

Decode creative item user data, route creative armor and offhand slots, accept their authoritative response slots, and replay clicks made before the player inventory opens. These corrections belong to the inventory flow introduced here.

Forward Java's mounted OPEN_INVENTORY command to the Bedrock Interact action with the mount runtime ID. The Java client sends this command when opening a storage vehicle while riding it; leaving it unhandled disconnects the client before Bedrock can open the container. Use the player runtime ID for an unmounted inventory request.

Capture immutable predicted slot identities when sending each authoritative request. Inventory-open contents can arrive before its successful response and overwrite a predicted item. Apply successful response counts and network IDs to the captured identity, while preserving server item metadata when an authoritative slot update already carries that response ID. Keep failure rollback revision checks separate. Canonicalize player-window snapshots to their shared physical inventory and retain sparse wire-slot mappings when copying containers.

Let the entity interaction transaction open unmounted storage vehicles. An extra OpenInventory Interact request suppresses the server container-open response on 1.26.51.1. Reconcile final custom names from successful stack responses, but preserve item damage because Bedrock reports a durability correction rather than an absolute damage value.

Open plain horse saddle and armor screens through Java's mount menu and route those slots through Bedrock HorseEquipContainer. Keep unsupported mount types closed until their layouts are verified. Assert the OpenInventory wire value accepted by the 1.26.51.1 server in the inventory interaction tests.

Expose Bedrock lecterns through the Java lectern menu using the block entity book and page state. Convert Bedrock two-page spreads to Java page buttons, derive the page count from the book when the block entity omits it, and send a lectern update only when the Bedrock spread changes. Schedule initial content after the menu opens and use a same-page update when Java closes it. Leave take-book disabled until its current Bedrock request semantics are verified.

Use the Bedrock beacon block entity Levels value for Java menu power, and refresh the open menu when block entity data changes. The container-open packet does not carry a strength field; reading server metadata also respects conditions beyond the local block palette.

Order hotbar drop actions as the container debit followed by the world
credit. Geyser recognizes this two-action normal transaction in that
order; the reversed sequence was ignored. Preserve single-item and
whole-stack counts without changing the tracked held stack.

Verified against protocol 2193 and Geyser 2.11.3's
BedrockInventoryTransactionTranslator at
https://github.com/GeyserMC/Geyser/blob/63a4e2b79b12f0d138777d5fd80176a112b4bd72/core/src/main/java/org/geysermc/geyser/translator/protocol/bedrock/BedrockInventoryTransactionTranslator.java
A focused transaction test checks action ordering and count conservation.

Accept the inventory, hotbar, and combined container aliases when reconciling
stack responses. Geyser returns hotbar names even for requests using the
combined name; ignoring those responses loses authoritative stack network
IDs. Validate slot bounds and reject dynamic container IDs as before.

Separate inventory-content indices from request slots at the container boundary. Protocol 2193 offhand contents occupy index zero, while requests and responses use equipment slot one. Translate both directions and preserve source and destination network IDs across cursor moves, swaps, quick moves, and drops. The mapping matches Geyser 2.11.3 PlayerInventoryTranslator at 63a4e2b79b12f0d138777d5fd80176a112b4bd72. Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 captures through official Geyser build 1247 confirmed the offhand slot and network-ID flow on October 1, 2026.

Use authoritative requests for hand swaps when negotiated by START_GAME. Queue outside-screen swaps behind a player inventory open and close that temporary inventory after acknowledgments finish, including delayed opens after a queued action expires. Suppress equipment echoes while predicting clicks so servers receive their own confirmed stack IDs. Classify queued screen interactions separately so later inventory features can retain independent writes when a screen closes.

Allocate legacy inventory transaction IDs with a separate negative even counter. Static analysis of the matching Bedrock 1.26.51.1 executable confirms allocation starts at -4 and decreases by two; item-stack requests use the independent odd sequence. Keep native captures and disassembly private.

Process temporary player inventory CLOSE acknowledgements without forwarding another Java screen close. Java owns its creative and player screen; forwarding the native acknowledgement prematurely closes it and causes subsequent inputs to affect the wrong UI. Keep genuine server-initiated closures intact.

Write stonecutter ingredients as typed HolderSet fields instead of raw
VarInts. Both encodings produce the same Java wire bytes, but ViaBackwards
reads HolderSet objects while translating UPDATE_RECIPES for older Java
clients. Raw fields disconnected Java 26.2 clients during Geyser login.
The regression test covers typed translation reads and direct wire bytes.

Use the shared Java slot resolver for pickup clicks. Small external
screens otherwise remap the first player inventory cells into the
player crafting or armor slots after selecting the inventory container.
A native 1.26.51.1 ViaProxy merchant test exposed the rejected placement;
keep all click modes on the same screen-relative slot mapping.

Refresh full item data after taking crafted output. ItemStackResponse supplies
counts, names and stack network IDs, but omits server-generated tags such as
map UUIDs and enchantments. An InventoryMismatch transaction asks the server
for its complete player inventory, cursor, armor and offhand state without
closing the workstation. Hold queued clicks until all snapshots arrive and
crafted destinations match the acknowledged IDs and counts. Reuse inventory
recovery if this refresh does not complete.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372, protocol 2193, confirms that mismatch
requests return all four inventory snapshots. Correlation tests cover map
identity, stale IDs and amounts, multiple destinations, inventory aliases,
and a later snapshot invalidating an earlier slot update. Raw probes stay
private. The target packet schema is documented at
https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-protocol-docs/1.26.51/packets/inventory-transaction-packet/

Request an InventoryMismatch refresh when timed-out requests enter recovery.
Reopening the inventory alone does not guarantee fresh cursor and equipment
snapshots. Use the same refresh transaction already verified against native
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 for crafted metadata, and retain the authoritative recovery
barrier before allowing more predictions.

Install complete inventory snapshots before notifying slot observers, and
notify only changed stacks using full Bedrock item equality. Counts and
stack network IDs remain authoritative. Repeated strict-BDS food updates
send a release transaction followed by unchanged inventory and offhand
snapshots; the old callback loop emits duplicate equipment packets and
exposes partially replaced contents to recipe observers.

Protocol-2193 MobEquipment describes changes to held slots or contents:
https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-protocol-docs/1.26.51/packets/mob-equipment-packet/
Keep real changes, including renewed network IDs, while suppressing
unchanged snapshot callbacks. Preserve single-slot update semantics for
existing in-place prediction. Tests exercise repeated snapshots, count
and ID changes, coherent callback contents, and array ownership. Native
item-use prediction and completion timing remain a separate requirement.

Handle UPDATE_EQUIP alongside CONTAINER_OPEN for horse, donkey and mule
screens without opening the same screen twice. Preserve native capacity,
content indices and request identities independently from Java menu slots.
Java retains a second equipment cell for donkeys and mules, so cargo slots
shift by one and player contents begin after the full Java slot array.
Use server equipment item and auxiliary-value restrictions for predictions
and quick moves. Preserve those slot mappings in rejection snapshots and
hide inaccessible cargo from unchested menus.

The protocol-2193 UPDATE_EQUIP schema defines byte container IDs, a signed
varint size, the actor unique ID and serialized data:
https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-protocol-docs/1.26.51/packets/update-equip-packet/
Geyser's mount opener sends size zero, while its chested translator maps
one native equipment slot and shifts cargo around Java's second slot:
https://github.com/GeyserMC/Geyser/blob/f66329d9d21b3c836edc014fbb9d8312fbe34285/core/src/main/java/org/geysermc/geyser/translator/inventory/horse/ChestedHorseInventoryTranslator.java
Use supported actor metadata and chest state instead of assuming that
UPDATE_EQUIP's size always describes native backing capacity.

An isolated headless official BDS 1.26.51.1, build 51061372, protocol 2193
probe confirms tamed mule capacity 16 before chest attachment, the CHESTED
flag after attachment, and Script API cargo items at indices 1 and 15.
It does not establish native screen-opening behavior or live Java request
parity. Unit tests cover packet registration and both opening orders,
equipment restrictions, hidden cells, all cargo/player mappings, native
network IDs, compact snapshots and rejection snapshot identity.
Keep proprietary captures private; full native, direct and ViaProxy mount
inventory comparisons remain required.

Reconcile protocol-2193 PlayerHotbar selections in the core inventory
before forwarding Java SET_HELD_SLOT. Subsequent use, drop, interaction,
and equipment packets must reference the server-selected stack. Keep
server-origin state updates separate from client equipment requests.
Validate the full Java slot value before narrowing it, so malformed
short values cannot alias a valid hotbar slot. Packet tests cover
repeated selections, unchanged inventory, ignored notifications,
unsigned wire bounds, and Java byte aliases.

Accept zero as a valid unsigned bundle storage identity. Require an exact
IntTag instead of aliasing absent or malformed bundle metadata to zero,
and share that identity lookup between item rendering and holder tracking.

An isolated official BDS 1.26.51.1 build 51061372, protocol 2193 probe
allocates dynamic bundle IDs zero and one. It accepts insertion, extraction
and compaction with ID zero, rejects a stale stack network ID, and accepts
a retry with the authoritative ID. Three tests cover isolated contents,
malformed identities and the complete unsigned ID representation. Keep
partial dynamic snapshots intact; full Java bundle UI parity remains
unverified. Raw server probes stay private.

Translate Java bundle selection and pickup actions through the dynamic
container registry, retaining zero IDs and native stack identities.
Generate merge reordering and compaction as accepted Swap, Place and Take
requests; preserve nested capacity and reject unavailable contents.
Restore rejected dynamic predictions by full container identity without
overwriting newer authoritative revisions. Keep prefix updates intact.

Official BDS 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 accepts the insertion, extraction,
merge, nesting and retry flows. Stateful tests exercise real click dispatch
and queued selection through a mocked packet transport boundary. Mockito
is test-only and uses its explicit agent to support current Java runtimes.
Graphical native and translated direct/proxy action verification remain
separate requirements.

Keep dynamic bundle state updates and authoritative revisions independent
from whether the bundle has a visible Java holder. Resolve nested bundles
to their outermost Java slot while keeping immediate dynamic request IDs
and native slots intact. Full external-menu refreshes include player slots.
Use SET_CURSOR_ITEM for dynamic cursor updates, and preserve HUD inventory
and crafting contents while separately refreshing an external menu cursor.
Java 26.3 ClientPacketListener treats menu-zero carried state independently
from the currently open menu, which made full HUD snapshots stale there.

Validate bundle references before recursive item conversion. Omit only the
optional contents component for cyclic or over-capacity nested references,
log the unsupported graph, and retain the item and authoritative state.
Per-conversion path and memoized counts bound shared graph expansion without
leaking state between updates. Each nested bundle contributes at least 1/16
of bundle capacity in the pinned Java BundleContents weight implementation;
17 empty container nodes form the representable capacity boundary. This is
a capacity guard, not a claim about the stream codec rejecting all metadata.

Stateful tests cover registered full and slot updates, deep/shared holders,
orphan attachment, current-menu cursors, native identities, revision-aware
rollback, cycles, missing parents and graph repair. A private probe with real
loaded mappings and production item rewriters reproduces the old rooted
cycle overflow, verifies authoritative update retention after omission,
and round-trips repaired 16-level contents through the Java 26.3 item codec.
Three negative controls against the previous production artifact fail on
the intended nested-refresh and revision assertions. These checks establish
conversion and packet behavior; graphical native/direct/proxy bundle parity
and native in-use drop timing remain separate requirements.

Delegate a single eligible Java quick-craft destination to ordinary pickup,
preserving the selected mouse mode. Java 26.3 AbstractContainerMenu resets
its drag state and invokes PICKUP when its selected slot set has size one.
Filter unresolved, output, equipment and incompatible slots before the
fallback; retain ordinary multi-slot distribution and request rollback.

An isolated strict official BDS 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 ViaProxy trace
confirms that right-clicking an empty slot with a cursor-held bundle sends
QUICK_CRAFT headers 4, 5 and 6, not a direct PICKUP packet. The valid HUD
bundle identity and registered contents survive all three headers, but
distribution placed the holder instead of extracting its contents. Tests
exercise the real click path, duplicate and invalid selections, empty
bundles, mismatched phases, queued rejection and retry, and ordinary drag
distribution. Native graphical parity and broader phase-one selection and
creative-clone behavior remain separate verification requirements.

Keep server-owned virtual menus independent of physical block anchors.
Bind a container only after its chunk is complete; known air denotes a
server-owned menu, while actor inventories ignore placeholder positions.
Retain block replacement and distance closure for verified physical
containers, and use a valid type title instead of container.null.

A live CubeCraft capture on Bedrock protocol 2193 confirms main-hand
right-click Use/Unknown/Failure reaches the server and opens an actor
inventory. The previous core closes it locally six milliseconds later.
Fix the container lifecycle and preserve the generic item-use contract.
Packet/state tests cover actors, known air, pending chunks, later physical
binding, physical distance closure, and authoritative menu actions.

Retain actual native opening fields and a connection-local generation
separately from translated menu type and physical binding. This lets
server-owned menus keep their identity through close/reopen and mount
opening order without fabricating packet fields or changing requests.

Adapt negotiated authored count-one controls to the real current menu's
server-authoritative inventory request engine. Derive source, permitted
destination, stack network IDs and capacity from server-owned state.
Require exact ordinary ChestContainer provider and opening lifetime;
retain the carried stack and existing prediction/response recovery.

The portable UI codec and scope stay in the upstream UI patch. This
backend, packet hooks and request-engine integration tests remain here
with the inventory translation tracked by ViaBedrock PR #276. Legacy
backend rejection must never block ordinary Java clicks.

Do not request a full inventory mismatch refresh for acknowledged ordinary
craft results without NBT. Their advertised item identity is already
complete and ItemStackResponse supplies the final count and network ID.
CubeCraft PoF on protocol 2193 acknowledges a plank craft but does not
supply the four complete snapshots required by our unconditional refresh;
the client then times out, holds cursor placement, and disconnects.
Retain the refresh for tagged or unknown identities, including generated
map UUIDs and enchantments. Tests cover ordinary cursor and inventory
results while existing map identity and snapshot correlation tests remain.

Preserve native MAX_STACK_SIZE from item_properties and component values. Target Geyser custom item schemas also expose icon textures.default and display_name.value. Skip post-ack identity refresh for exact creative palette output: Geyser ignores INVENTORY_MISMATCH, so that unnecessary recipe synchronization gate can stall later inventory actions. Targeted tests preserve the refresh gate for actual crafted identity changes.

Snapshot original container identity once per click without probing slot zero, which is invalid for crafting and other mapped native menus. Keep named snapshot lookup limited to dynamic registry containers so closed menus cannot resolve to an unrelated HUD. Runtime reproduction and action/rollback tests cover crafting input, player inventory, and bundle prediction.

Choose native storage screen families from the Bedrock container type and
bound block tag, retaining barrel, shulker and ender-chest variants. The
version-3 channel transports added families only to capable add-ons.
Generalize count-one manual actions to the same ordinary storage providers
using their actual request-slot identities, sizes and live menu generation.
Reject workstations and stale or mismatched menu identities. Closing and
capability withdrawal continue to retire the current action scope.

Preserve each physical storage variant's default title and localize native
labels independently of raw authored title conditions. The licensed pinned
1.26.51.1 language asset defines container.barrel, container.shulkerbox and
container.enderchest; custom names retain their original localization policy.

Retain ContainerSetData properties on their genuine container before
narrowing them for Java compatibility. The Mojang 1.26.51-beta.1 schema
for protocol 2193 encodes both property ID and value as signed compressed
int32 fields. Preserve unknown IDs, full values and absent versus zero
without assigning uncertain furnace properties a new meaning.

Keep property revisions separate from item revisions. Rejected item
requests restore item snapshots without rolling back newer server
progress, and reopening a reused window ID starts with fresh properties.
Packet-handler tests cover signed boundaries, Java projection, unknown
properties and closing; snapshot and rejection tests cover independent
state. Native cooking scenes and direct/ViaProxy native comparisons
remain incomplete; this state retention does not establish UI parity.

Reconcile authored count-one requests from the slots reported by the server.
The target protocol-2193 Social response can acknowledge only an empty
cursor without confirming the predicted transfer. Restore omitted
predictions only while their owner, opening generation, item revision and
contents still match. Preserve newer authoritative updates and reject late
responses to reused menus. Retire clicks queued against restored contents.
Keep ordinary Java request prediction unchanged. Six regression cases
cover complete and partial acknowledgments, inventory aliases, lifecycle
reuse and newer state alongside the existing inventory tests.

Apply request menu-lifetime checks to ordinary predictions as well as
reported-slot authored actions. A late response identifies its request,
not a newly opened container that happens to accept the same slot name.
Reject success updates and rejection snapshots for replaced menus,
including a reopened instance of the same container object.

Keep permanent player inventory and cursor reconciliation after a screen
transition. Scope the other player-UI cells to the captured menu lifetime:
a closed workstation response must not overwrite a later HUD crafting or
output cell through named-slot fallback. Do not start crafted metadata
refresh for those obsolete destinations.

The expanded ordinary-click regression fails on the previous code by
overwriting a reopened chest. Tests cover both response policies, success
and rejection, object and ID reuse, workstation-to-HUD alias fallback,
persistent inventory/cursor controls and obsolete crafted destinations.
These are translator lifecycle tests, not a new native response-manager
or graphical parity claim. Fixtures capture predictions at the same
boundary used by the production packet sender.

Keep dynamic bundle replies bound to their captured registry object.
Registry cleanup and reuse of the same dynamic ID must not redirect an
old success response or rejection snapshot onto the replacement bundle.
The regression fails before this identity guard; existing active-bundle
controls retain authoritative refresh and rollback behavior.

Capture the latest opened-menu generation even when no screen is open.
The active-menu generation resets to zero on closure, so using it alone
misses an intervening open/close cycle. A focused failing-before control
covers replies sent with no menu before another menu opens and closes,
while retaining cursor and permanent inventory reconciliation.

Present the target brewing layout through a versioned workstation channel.
Keep ingredient, fuel, and bottle actions on the existing authoritative
Java menu path; storage count-one providers do not apply to workstations.
Send native counters as signed int32 snapshots scoped to the presentation
sequence so Java short projection cannot corrupt the authored indicators.
Require both workstation and counter channels before replacing a screen.

Correct brewing input and fuel addressing: BDS 1.26.51.1 exposes the
ingredient at native slot 0 and fuel at native slot 4. Match the existing
block-entity projection and route both content and requests consistently
to Java ingredient slot 3 and fuel slot 4. The previous mapping displayed
an ingredient in the fuel cell and could not start an ordinary brew.
The slot/collection regression fails before this correction.

Resolve item identities through ordered native registry passes. Keep the
constructed factory independent of the latest runtime-ID owner, including
invalid-ID updates and first explicit version selection. Own component tags
so later consumers cannot alter the registry snapshot.

Replace the static maximum-stack table and resource-pack size override with
constructor defaults and typed compiled updates for the pinned Bedrock
1.26.51.1 protocol 2193. Preserve native unsigned-byte zero and high values
for inventory capacity, narrowing only the Java component to 1..99. Reject
zero-capacity bundle weights without dividing by zero. Retain native food
and block traits for subsequent cooking-category evaluation.

Private native fixtures cover 26 component updates and 93 legacy cases,
with persistent update sequences. Their executable SHA-256 and scope are
recorded in Data Asset Sources.md. Factory ownership, signed runtime IDs,
tag snapshots, category precedence and typed selectors have semantic tests.
This does not establish graphical parity or complete cooking providers.

Share native concrete recipe descriptor identity and connection registry
ownership. Separate raw network construction from conversion of an already
constructed stack: runtime block ownership can replace wildcard metadata,
associated block items can resolve through another numeric lookup key, and
ordinary items with a block association retain their own Item definition.
Preserve ushort IDs, signed auxiliary normalization and native invalid
lookup behavior instead of grouping recipes by received item metadata.

A fresh Bedrock1.26.51.1 build51061372 executable reference covers3,600
complete ItemStack-to-descriptor conversions, numeric registry lookups,
block/wildcard constructors and descriptor hashes. Tests preserve result
identity, lookup order, absent definitions and runtime-ID reassignment.
Numeric registry ownership and stack fields are supplied probe inputs;
full raw network construction and block fallback integration remain open.

Compare the network recipe-key API against 2,250 fresh executions of the
complete parsed native ItemStack constructor followed by descriptor and
hash conversion. The fixture supplies warm associated defaults/caches,
registry/palette storage and an empty user-data load boundary; actual
built-in/archetype initialization and cold auxiliary mapping stay open.

Resolve named recipe ingredients through the connection Item registry and
construct candidate descriptor identity through the native network-header
rules. Compare associated block states by exact palette ownership rather
than auxiliary-number equality. Native matching accepts different metadata
that resolve to the same Block, and recognizes the wildcard on either
side. Narrow named metadata to signed-short storage before clamping it;
negative one is not the native wildcard. Keep packet metadata unchanged.

An executable fixture of Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 verifies 240
named-construction, deferred-resolution and concrete-comparison cases.
It executes the actual captured canonical Item registry lookup and
query-first comparison, with warm supplied BlockLegacy auxiliary caches
and allocator/CRT/TLS/lock boundaries. Alias initialization, binary packet
parsing and native UI remain outside that fixture. This connects native descriptor conversion to production recipe matching.
Native cooking-source collection remains follow-up work.

Construct network Items before user-data loading. Both codecs pass raw
signed-short auxiliary bits, the normalized byte count and unresolved
runtime ID to the connection registry. Prefer a runtime Block only when
its numeric ownership resolves to the original Item, then resolve the
associated Legacy lazily. Retain exact runtime slots independently of
canonical auxiliary descriptors, including unmodeled archetypes.

Move network header selection out of the recipe-only helper. Recipe
matching consumes the constructed Item without reconstructing it, and
outgoing codecs use the native concrete descriptor ID and metadata. Keep
outer stack network IDs when the Item is empty; omit its user data.
A zero count clears Item/Block ownership, but the block-item constructor
can reapply wildcard auxiliary metadata after that invalidation.

Compare both complete codec paths with 2,250 native parsed-construction
results, including Item ID, metadata, count, Block ownership and recipe
keys/hashes. Compare both outgoing headers with 384 complete native
NetworkItemStackDescriptor producer executions. The references supply
registry/palette storage, warm auxiliary caches and runtime seams; actual
item installation, cold unmodeled archetypes, specialized user-data load
callbacks and saved-NBT construction remain unverified by these fixtures.

A zero user-data marker omits only NBT. Continue decoding both restriction
arrays and shield blocking ticks, matching the target 1.26.51.1 generic
reader 1424d06e0 and shield reader 143043940. Returning at the marker lost
state needed to distinguish cooking outputs and item behavior. Both
network item layouts share this loader and retain partial-tail behavior.
Positive legacy NBT markers remain outside this correction.

Share the native transfer quantity decoder across pickup and authored
count-one request paths. Preserve explicit-count bounds, the separate
all/half/one descriptor domains, unsigned-byte narrowing, and rejection
of unknown descriptor values. Source validity, maximum-stack resolution,
permissions and destination capacity remain separate executor steps.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372, protocol 2193, establishes the decoder
at 1433e8615 within transfer executor 1433e8380, including the out-of-line
one branch at 1433e8928. A private fixture executes the original decoder
instructions for 1,638 descriptor and count cases. This proves this
internal count boundary, not complete recipe dispatch or native UI parity.

Reject native count-one sources without a positive server stack network
ID and skip unresolved occupied merge destinations. Do not unbox a
missing ID or send a predicted request with an unresolved identity.
Keep empty destinations at network ID zero and derive predicted counts
from the actual emitted action.

Pass the connection-owned native stack query matcher into authored
count-one placement. Use the destination as query, matching the ordinary
native transfer comparator rather than Java tag object equality. Keep
modern network-ID and placement-permission checks in this authority
adapter; the shared planner contains no raw-equality fallback.

Retain separately scoped ordinary transfer requests as one pending input
batch. Capture each intermediate prediction, buffer responses until the
batch is complete, then restore the oldest snapshots and replay accepted
deltas in request order. This preserves confirmed stack IDs when a later
transfer rejects and keeps queued clicks behind the complete batch.
Server revisions and menu generations still protect newer contents and
replacement menus. Craft-result requests retain their separate full-item
synchronization path.

The pinned native 1.26.51.1 client control emits two separately owned
requests for one two-source cooking input when no enclosing request is
open. Native flush sends both in one packet; the second destination uses
the first request ID. The configuration registry defaults
`enable_client_container_simulation` to false, and the original cooking
initializer leaves its simulation helper absent under that gate. Full
controller/model construction and independent frame scopes remain open.
This batch API supports those request boundaries without claiming that the
authored cooking backend or full native application grouping is finished.

Record the server revision when each batch response arrives. Discard its
stale slot acknowledgments if newer authoritative contents arrive before
the final response. This preserves packet ordering across the batch's
buffering boundary instead of allowing delayed reconciliation to replace
newer server state.

Submit prepared batches through the request tracker and restore their
oldest snapshots if packet submission throws. Release pending ownership
and queued input on that failure so an unsent batch cannot lock inventory.

Ignore empty resource-pack display-name overrides when selecting the Java
fallback label. Retain upper-pack precedence across primitive and object
forms. Network component descriptions now belong to ItemRegistry, whose
construction and ordered-reader state also drives mapped Java items. Keep
this loader focused on its remaining icon, cooldown and use metadata.

The registry-owned name checks cover all 13 prior typed update results and
the newer construction/admission controls. Remove the superseded network
name reader and its three tests so two independent state machines cannot
drift. Native asset precedence and full ordinary names remain open.

Own player inventory openings by the actual installed client screen over a negotiated payload shared by direct and proxy routes. Java reuses menu zero, so retain retired native opens until acknowledgment and correlate server closes to a monotonic client opening. Serialize rapid reopenings, discard retired screen clicks, and preserve later global inventory interactions. Reject malformed or stale lifecycle messages without changing native ownership.

Verify nonempty component readers inside complete native registry and
late-block construction. Standalone reader controls cannot establish how
later registration, malformed updates and raw-name lookup affect retained
owners. Two original protocol2193 controls cover 22 declarations, including
retained flag state, exact-name admission, staged replacement and twelve
native property receivers. Their packaged comparisons match all 8,540
values and complete native input/registry/TLS/static/CRT cleanup.

These controls retain the existing default Experiments and base-game
version1.26.50 context. Complete component types, custom blocks, ItemStack
construction and installed player screens remain separate boundaries.

Resolve named recipe ingredients through constructed definition identities
and their receiver-owned block associations. Keep numeric ownership,
metadata aliases and direct alias-member lookup separate. A collision can
select a different numeric owner while the named recipe still uses its
original definition and associated block.

Target 1.26.51.1 resolver 142522910 narrows auxiliary values before saved-name
lookup and uses BlockLegacy numeric ownership for block fallback.
